1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,810
Why isn't 1/2 + 1/3
equal to 2/5?

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:07,800
Let's draw 1/2 + 1/3 as bars

3
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:10,930
Add the tops and the bottoms —
is it 2/5?

4
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:13,400
Let's draw a line at 1/2…

5
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:15,570
Huh? It's smaller than 1/2!

6
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:18,140
Adding can't make it smaller —
2/5 is wrong

7
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:20,710
A 1/2 piece and a 1/3 piece
are different sizes

8
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:22,880
You can't just count pieces
of different sizes

9
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:26,850
Cut both into 6 pieces

10
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:29,220
1/2 = 3/6, 1/3 = 2/6
Now the pieces are the same size

11
00:00:29,270 --> 00:00:35,620
Put the same-size pieces together: 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6

12
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:39,120
Make the pieces the same size first —
that is finding a common denominator

13
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:43,870
NEXT: Why do we flip and multiply when dividing by a fraction?
